## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

A scalable Angular architecture splits code into core (root-provided, app-wide singleton services), shared (reusable UI with no business logic), and feature (self-contained, lazy-loaded chunks that can declare their own locally-scoped services). Lazy loading via `loadComponent` keeps the initial bundle small since a feature's code only downloads when the user navigates there, and component-level `providers` arrays keep feature-local state from leaking outside that feature or persisting between visits — verified live by watching a feature-scoped service get a fresh instance on every remount, while a root-provided core service stayed the same single instance throughout.

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

For a scalable enterprise Angular app, I'd organize code into three categories. Core holds app-wide singleton services — auth state, logging, global configuration — registered with `providedIn: 'root'`, so Angular's injector creates exactly one instance for the app's entire lifetime and hands that same instance to every consumer. Shared holds dumb, reusable UI — components, pipes, directives with no business logic and no dependency on any specific feature's services — things like a card component or a date-formatting pipe that any feature can safely reuse. Feature modules own a specific piece of functionality end to end, and critically, each one is lazy-loaded through the router using `loadComponent` (for standalone components) with a dynamic `import()`, so its code only ships to the browser the first time a user actually navigates there, keeping the initial bundle small.

The part that's easy to get wrong is service scoping within features. If a feature needs its own local service — say, a data-fetching facade specific to a dashboard — the right move is usually to declare it in that feature's own component `providers` array rather than `providedIn: 'root'`. I verified this distinction directly: a root-provided `ActivityLogService` stayed the same single instance across every navigation, exactly as a core singleton should. Meanwhile, a `DashboardDataService` declared only in `DashboardComponent`'s own `providers` array got a completely fresh instance every time I navigated away and back to that route, because the router destroys and recreates the lazy-loaded component (and everything in its provider array) on each visit. That's the correct behavior for feature-local state — it shouldn't leak into other features, and it shouldn't silently persist stale data between visits.

Beyond that, a production setup would add router guards for auth-gated routes, a preloading strategy so features load in the background after the initial paint instead of only on-demand, and resolvers for data that must be ready before a route activates. But the core structural decision — core singletons vs. shared dumb UI vs. lazy-loaded, independently-scoped features — is what makes the codebase navigable and the bundle size manageable as the app and the team both grow.

## 12. Quick Revision

Core = `providedIn: 'root'` singletons (auth, logging, config) — one instance, whole app, forever. Shared = dumb reusable UI, no feature logic, no feature service injection. Feature = lazy-loaded via `loadComponent`/dynamic `import()`, keeps initial bundle small, can declare its own component-scoped `providers` for feature-local state. Component-scoped providers get a fresh instance every time the component (re)mounts — verified live: a feature service's instance count incremented on every remount while a root-provided service stayed at instance #1 the whole time. Trap: don't default everything to `providedIn: 'root'` — that turns feature-local state into an accidental app-wide singleton.

## 13. Memory Trick

**"Core is the one thing that never leaves. Shared doesn't know whose party it's at. Feature packs its own bags every time it shows up."**
